1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:02,888
Your Mac is off.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:04,651
Almost.

3
00:00:04,651 --> 00:00:10,003
Somewhere inside, a few tiny circuits are
still awake — waiting for a single press.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,742
And when it comes, a cold slab of silicon
turns itself into a working computer,

5
00:00:15,742 --> 00:00:17,123
in a matter of seconds.

6
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:18,215
Firmware.

7
00:00:18,215 --> 00:00:19,035
Bootloaders.

8
00:00:19,035 --> 00:00:19,650
A kernel.

9
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:22,997
Hundreds of programs, each
one starting the next.

10
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:26,475
And not one of them is trusted
until it's been checked.

11
00:00:27,067 --> 00:00:30,141
Because whoever controls
the very first instruction…

12
00:00:30,141 --> 00:00:32,507
controls everything that comes after it.

13
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:36,387
Today: what really happens when
you press the power button.

14
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,873
The press closes a tiny switch,

15
00:00:47,873 --> 00:00:51,376
and wakes a power-management
chip that never fully sleeps.

16
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:56,624
It switches on the processor's power
supplies one at a time, in a strict order —

17
00:00:56,624 --> 00:01:00,217
waiting for each voltage to
settle before starting the next.

18
00:01:00,667 --> 00:01:04,613
Get the order wrong, and the chip
could misbehave — or even be damaged.

19
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:10,373
A quartz crystal starts to vibrate —
the 24 MHz heartbeat from Episode One —

20
00:01:10,373 --> 00:01:11,969
and the clocks lock on.

21
00:01:12,467 --> 00:01:14,604
Then the reset signal is released.

22
00:01:14,604 --> 00:01:18,375
A single processor core wakes
up, knowing exactly one thing:

23
00:01:18,375 --> 00:01:20,637
where to find its first instruction.

24
00:01:24,333 --> 00:01:29,537
That instruction lives in the Boot ROM: a
small program etched into the chip itself,

25
00:01:29,537 --> 00:01:30,590
when it was made.

26
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:32,077
It can never be changed.

27
00:01:32,533 --> 00:01:37,454
That's its strength, and its weakness:
if a flaw were ever found in a Boot ROM,

28
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:40,568
no software update could
fix it — only a new chip.

29
00:01:40,568 --> 00:01:43,371
So it's kept as small
and simple as possible.

30
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:46,552
Built into it is a key
— Apple's public key.

31
00:01:46,552 --> 00:01:49,115
Everything that follows is anchored to it.

32
00:01:49,567 --> 00:01:53,978
The Boot ROM's job is to load the
next stage, the Low-Level Bootloader,

33
00:01:53,978 --> 00:01:56,649
from a small flash chip
on the logic board.

34
00:01:57,033 --> 00:02:01,620
But before it runs a single instruction
of it, the Boot ROM checks its signature.

35
00:02:02,933 --> 00:02:03,581
Here's how.

36
00:02:03,581 --> 00:02:08,523
The code is run through a hash function,
which boils it down to a short fingerprint.

37
00:02:08,867 --> 00:02:12,707
Change even a single bit, and the
fingerprint changes completely.

38
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,384
Apple signs that fingerprint with a
private key that never leaves Apple.

39
00:02:17,384 --> 00:02:22,032
The public key in the Boot ROM can check
the signature — but it can't forge one.

40
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:25,285
If the check fails, the
Mac refuses to start,

41
00:02:25,285 --> 00:02:28,037
and waits for another
computer to restore it.

42
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:30,570
If it passes, control moves on.

43
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:35,243
And from here on, every stage checks
the next one before handing over.

44
00:02:35,243 --> 00:02:37,780
A chain of trust, anchored in silicon.

45
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:46,610
The Low-Level Bootloader starts out in a
tiny scratchpad of memory inside the chip —

46
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:49,012
because the main memory isn't ready yet.

47
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:54,664
It verifies and loads firmware for the
chip's helper processors: storage, display,

48
00:02:54,664 --> 00:02:56,589
power management, Thunderbolt.

49
00:02:57,067 --> 00:03:00,954
It trains the DRAM — sending
test patterns back and forth,

50
00:03:00,954 --> 00:03:04,640
fine-tuning the timing until
every bit arrives cleanly.

51
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:09,725
And it reads the boot policy: a small
file, signed by the Secure Enclave,

52
00:03:09,725 --> 00:03:12,597
that records the security
settings you chose.

53
00:03:13,067 --> 00:03:18,240
That policy is also the only way to start
something other than macOS, like Linux:

54
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:23,541
you have to lower the security setting
yourself, from recovery, with your password.

55
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:27,838
Then it hands over to iBoot
— the main bootloader,

56
00:03:27,838 --> 00:03:31,084
which belongs to the version
of macOS you're starting.

57
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:35,193
iBoot loads a map of all the
hardware, called the device tree,

58
00:03:35,193 --> 00:03:38,945
and the kernel itself — checking
every signature along the way.

59
00:03:39,367 --> 00:03:44,650
Meanwhile, the Secure Enclave boots its own
operating system, from its own Boot ROM,

60
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:46,663
walled off from everything else.

61
00:03:47,133 --> 00:03:50,262
Finally, iBoot checks the
seal on the system volume…

62
00:03:50,262 --> 00:03:51,827
and jumps into the kernel.

63
00:03:55,633 --> 00:03:57,934
That seal protects macOS itself.

64
00:03:57,934 --> 00:04:03,975
The operating system lives on a read-only
volume, where every file has its own hash.

65
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:08,376
Those hashes are hashed again,
and again, all the way up a tree,

66
00:04:08,376 --> 00:04:10,255
into a single value: the seal.

67
00:04:10,700 --> 00:04:14,305
Change one byte, in one file,
anywhere — and the seal breaks.

68
00:04:14,767 --> 00:04:16,690
And it isn't checked just once.

69
00:04:16,690 --> 00:04:19,854
Every time the kernel reads
from the system volume,

70
00:04:19,854 --> 00:04:22,212
it hashes the data again and compares.

71
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:27,350
That's why even malware with administrator
rights can't quietly rewrite the system.

72
00:04:27,350 --> 00:04:28,695
It would break the seal.

73
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:31,855
Now the kernel is in charge.

74
00:04:31,855 --> 00:04:37,155
First, it switches on virtual memory,
giving itself — and every future program —

75
00:04:37,155 --> 00:04:38,811
its own map of addresses.

76
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:43,445
It wakes the other processor cores,
one by one, and starts the scheduler.

77
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:48,091
It walks the device tree and matches
each piece of hardware with a driver.

78
00:04:48,533 --> 00:04:52,923
Every system program it starts must
match a list of approved fingerprints —

79
00:04:52,923 --> 00:04:55,616
the trust cache that
iBoot loaded and checked.

80
00:04:56,067 --> 00:04:58,264
Then it creates the very first program.

81
00:05:02,067 --> 00:05:03,866
Process number one: launchd.

82
00:05:03,866 --> 00:05:07,400
Every other program on your
Mac will be its descendant.

83
00:05:07,867 --> 00:05:10,804
It reads hundreds of
small configuration files,

84
00:05:10,804 --> 00:05:15,617
and starts the system's background
services — logging, networking, Bluetooth,

85
00:05:15,617 --> 00:05:18,555
search — many of them
only when they're needed.

86
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,736
Within seconds, hundreds of processes are
running — before you've even logged in.

87
00:05:24,167 --> 00:05:27,958
One of them is the WindowServer,
which takes over the display.

88
00:05:27,958 --> 00:05:29,671
Another is the login window.

89
00:05:30,967 --> 00:05:32,257
You type your password.

90
00:05:32,257 --> 00:05:34,615
Your Mac never stores the password itself.

91
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,152
With FileVault on,

92
00:05:36,152 --> 00:05:40,888
the Secure Enclave combines it with a
secret key fused into this one chip,

93
00:05:40,888 --> 00:05:43,576
to unlock the keys that protect your data.

94
00:05:44,033 --> 00:05:46,432
Your files were encrypted the whole time.

95
00:05:46,432 --> 00:05:47,660
Now they can be read.

96
00:05:48,167 --> 00:05:52,178
launchd starts your session: the
menu bar, the Dock, the Finder…

97
00:05:52,178 --> 00:05:53,244
and your desktop.

98
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:56,249
So, from the top.

99
00:05:56,733 --> 00:05:57,306
A press.

100
00:05:57,306 --> 00:05:58,666
Power, in sequence.

101
00:05:58,666 --> 00:06:01,100
A crystal, a clock, a single core.

102
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:03,606
The Boot ROM checks the bootloader.

103
00:06:03,606 --> 00:06:05,611
The bootloader checks iBoot.

104
00:06:05,611 --> 00:06:07,329
iBoot checks the kernel.

105
00:06:07,329 --> 00:06:10,551
The kernel checks every
system file it reads.

106
00:06:10,551 --> 00:06:11,123
launchd.

107
00:06:11,123 --> 00:06:12,340
The login window.

108
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:12,627
You.

109
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:15,487
Every link verified before it runs.

110
00:06:15,487 --> 00:06:20,124
A chain of trust, forged from scratch,
every time you press that button.

111
00:06:21,033 --> 00:06:22,147
Next time: memory.

112
00:06:22,147 --> 00:06:27,156
Where does all this data actually live —
and how does the chip get to it so fast?
